+### Differential Object Filter
+An invisible object that makes overlapping volume parts invisible [S4]. In PovRay rendering, it interacts with other objects. In preview, it interacts only with volume [S4]. Rendering quality must be set quite high because the volume shape becomes complicated rather than a simple box [S4].
+
+### FXAA (Fast Approximate Antialiasing)
+An antialiasing method that operates on the final color buffer. It performs edge detection and applies perceptually correct edge filtering. It provides antialiasing for both geometry and volume in the composited image [S5]. FXAA is applied to the final composited image, whereas multisampled G-buffer antialiasing is applied during geometry rendering. They can be combined for improved quality [S5].
+
+### Inverse Diffuse Filter
+Applies inverse diffusion with a negative diffusion coefficient to correct z-direction blur in confocal microscopy data [S4]. Works only in preview. Filtered data must be exported to new .df3 files for PovRay rendering [S4].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of filtering in volume rendering has evolved alongside hardware capabilities. Early methods relied on simple box filters for crisp edges in high-resolution volumes, while Gaussian filters were introduced to prevent banding artifacts [S1]. The introduction of splatting techniques, such as those by Zwicker et al., allowed for efficient anti-aliasing of unstructured grids by projecting 3D kernels into 2D ellipsoids [S1, citing [5]]. More recent developments include adaptive prefiltering for participating media to handle singularity artifacts [S1] and screen-space bilateral filters for preserving edges in post-processing [S1].
